Title: "Axiom Systems and Size"
Id: "4183ca80-b7a4-47be-9619-0f46dafc8553"


Text: "The elementary constructors established in previous sections—pairing,
       union, and power set—form only the scaffolding of modern set theory. To
       develop higher mathematics, analysis, and transfinite ordinal arithmetic,
       Zermelo--Fraenkel set theory incorporates powerful principles governing
       how predicates select elements from existing sets, how functional
       assignments generate new collections, and how ill-founded pathologies are
       excluded.

       Because first-order logic cannot quantify over arbitrary properties or
       predicates, principles such as Separation and Replacement cannot be
       expressed as single axioms. Instead, they are formulated as *axiom
       schemas*—infinite families of axioms, supplying one formal postulate for
       every well-formed formula $\varphi$ in the language $\mathcal{L}_{\in}$."
Id: "ddb95a61-dddb-4c5a-bb15-430be7091d5a"


SectionTitle: "Separation and Replacement"
Id: "b04301f4-2e38-4797-b6cd-626d4be8bcb7"


Text: "To prevent Russell's paradox while preserving the ability to define sets
       by properties, Ernst Zermelo replaced naive comprehension with the Axiom
       Schema of Separation. Rather than forming collections from thin air,
       Separation allows a definite mathematical property to carve out a subset
       from an already existing set:"
Id: "43390b84-bd99-411d-873e-aa658d157974"


Axiom:
given:
. A is \set
. phi(x_) is (_ is \set) -> (_ is \\statement)
then:
. exists: B is \set
  suchThat:
  . forAll: x is \set
    then:
    . have: x "in" B
      iff:
      . x "in"? A
      . phi(x)
Documented:
. called: "Axiom Schema of Separation"
. description: "First-order schema asserting that for any formula $\varphi(x)$,
                $\{x \in A : \varphi(x)\}$ is a set. Restricts comprehension to
                subcollections of existing sets, preventing Russell's paradox."
Id: "5077fbc7-a5c8-4d60-b1a7-a9d65097f003"


Text: "While Separation is conservative—it can never create sets of greater rank
       than the set from which elements are carved—advanced mathematics requires
       assembling the full image of a set under a definable functional
       assignment. The Axiom Schema of Replacement, formulated by Abraham
       Fraenkel and Thoralf Skolem, guarantees that the image of any set under a
       class function is again a set:"
Id: "5b27a9c9-14c9-4070-8360-1adc66d8a79f"


Axiom:
given:
. A is \set
. phi(x_, y_) is (_ is \set, _ is \set) -> (_ is \\statement)
where:
. forAll: x "in" A
  then:
  . existsUnique: y is \set
    suchThat: phi(x, y)
then:
. exists: B is \set
  suchThat:
  . forAll: y is \set
    then:
    . have: y "in" B
      iff:
      . exists: x "in" A
        suchThat: phi(x, y)
Documented:
. called: "Axiom Schema of Replacement"
. description: "First-order schema asserting that the image $F[A]$ of a set
                under any functional class operation is a set. Vital for
                transfinite induction and forming limit ordinals such as
                $\omega+\omega$ and $\aleph_\omega$."
References:
. $enderton.set.theory:page{179}
Id: "6e09ea04-f1dd-41c0-a0f5-4c962cd819ac"


Text: "The distinction between Separation and Replacement is foundational.
       Separation can only construct subsets of an existing set, remaining
       strictly within its rank. Replacement, by contrast, is an expansive
       generative principle: it allows transfinite recursion to step across
       limit stages, ensuring that sequences indexed by ordinals (such as
       $\aleph_\omega = \bigcup_{n < \omega} \aleph_n$) cohere into genuine
       sets."
Id: "1cbdad45-8bc2-4388-8a12-19b187499517"


SectionTitle: "Foundation and Choice"
Id: "960e9966-e880-421a-afa0-c8c753476a7c"


Text: "To exclude pathological circularities such as sets containing themselves
       ($x \in x$) or infinite descending membership chains, Zermelo--Fraenkel
       set theory includes the Axiom of Foundation (or Regularity), ensuring
       that the membership relation is well-founded:"
Id: "b37d951b-845d-43a7-8047-7e6c18a0c843"


Axiom:
given: A is \(nonempty)::set
then:
. exists: a is \set
  suchThat:
  . a "in"? A
  . not:
    . exists: x is \set
      suchThat:
      . x "in"? A
      . x "in"? a
Documented:
. called: "Axiom of Foundation"
. description: "The regularity postulate: every nonempty set $A$ contains an
                $\in$-minimal element ($a \cap A = \emptyset$). Prohibits
                infinite descending $\in$-chains $\dots \in x_2 \in x_1 \in x_0$
                and self-containing sets $x \in x$."
References:
. $enderton.set.theory:page{206}
Id: "cc3fc1c3-8ea9-45a6-874c-9215525952f4"


Text: "Finally, mathematics requires a principle for making infinitely many
       arbitrary selections simultaneously. Formulated by Ernst Zermelo in 1904,
       the Axiom of Choice guarantees that every family of nonempty sets admits
       a choice function that selects an element from each member:"
Id: "1f78bcd7-a146-4c2b-be56-cca4a18411cc"


Axiom:
given:
. I, U is \set
. A(i__) is \function:on{I}:to{\power.set:of{U}}
where:
. forAll: i "in" I
  then:
  . exists: S is \set
    suchThat:
    . S = A(i)
    . S != \empty.set
then:
. exists: f(i__) is \function:on{I}:to{U}
  suchThat:
  . forAll: i "in" I
    then:
    . exists: S is \set
      suchThat:
      . S = A(i)
      . f(i) "in"? S
Documented:
. called: "Axiom of Choice"
. description: "The non-constructive choice postulate: every family of nonempty
                pairwise disjoint sets admits a transversal selector. Guarantees
                that every Cartesian product of nonempty sets is nonempty, and
                every surjection has a right inverse."
References:
. $enderton.set.theory:page{151}
. $halmos.set.theory:page{59}
Id: "3f1ec40e-207c-431e-918e-7a8f4f23fa46"


Text: "Over the base theory ZF, the Axiom of Choice is equivalent to numerous
       core structural principles that permeate classical mathematics:"
Id: "83bfdbdc-942f-4d36-bb15-abee8ec6e6ba"


Text: "**Theorem.** Over the base theory ZF, the Axiom of Choice (AC) is
       equivalent to a vast constellation of fundamental mathematical principles
       across algebra, topology, and analysis. Among the most prominent are:
       1. *Zermelo's Well-Ordering Theorem*: Every set can be endowed with a
          well-ordering.
       2. *Zorn's Lemma*: Every non-empty partially ordered set in which every
          chain possesses an upper bound contains at least one maximal element.
       3. *Right Inverses*: Every surjective function admits a right inverse (a
          section).
       4. *Linear Algebra*: Every vector space possesses a Hamel basis.
       5. *Topology*: Tychonoff's Theorem, asserting that any product of compact
          topological spaces is compact.

       The equivalence of these formulations permits mathematicians to invoke
       Choice in whichever structural guise is best suited to the problem at
       hand."
Id: "694788bb-a939-406b-8a3f-62730d8d0399"


SectionTitle: "Cardinality and Cantor's Theorem"
Id: "ce8978c6-895a-4528-bc27-a1796c5fc751"


Text: "The concept of size for infinite sets cannot rely on counting elements.
       In 1874, Georg Cantor established that two sets have the same size—or
       cardinality—if and only if their elements can be placed in exact
       one-to-one correspondence:"
Id: "c80e9001-f0e8-4308-bffd-d6b0d40fd2ac"


[A \.equinumerous.with./ B]
States:
when: A, B is \set
that:
. exists: f(x__) is \function:on{A}:to{B}
  suchThat: f is? \(bijective)::function:?on{A}:?to{B}
Documented:
. called: "$A?$ and $B?$ have the same cardinality"
. written: "|A?| = |B?|"
. description: "Cardinal equinumerosity $|A| = |B|$: existence of an invertible
                set isomorphism (bijection) $f: A \to B$ witness to identical
                cardinality in Set."
References:
. $enderton.set.theory:page{132}
Id: "5ba55513-d2b3-458e-afd9-2fbd7507d47f"


Text: "Similarly, one set is at most as large as another if it can be embedded
       injectively into it:"
Id: "0ee0a342-4975-42ba-816a-c38368bc5e4d"


[A \.cardinality.leq./ B]
States:
when: A, B is \set
that:
. exists: f(x__) is \function:on{A}:to{B}
  suchThat: f is? \(injective)::function:?on{A}:?to{B}
Documented:
. called: "cardinality of $A?$ is at most cardinality of $B?$"
. written: "|A?| \leq |B?|"
. description: "Cardinal sub-dominance $|A| \le |B|$: existence of an injective
                embedding $f: A \hookrightarrow B$, establishing a preorder on
                cardinalities."
References:
. $enderton.set.theory:page{132}
Id: "d3cc912e-a944-4a5f-8cc8-cca18c511746"


Text: "Using initial segments of the natural numbers as standard measuring rods,
       a set is finite if it can be put in bijection with some
       $\{0, 1, \dots, n-1\}$:"
Id: "06972eb0-e907-4e46-a5b0-1487c82006b5"


[\(finite)::set]
Refines: X
satisfies:
. exists: n is \natural
  suchThat: X \.equinumerous.with./ \natural.initial.segment:before{n}
Documented:
. adjective: "finite"
. description: "Dedekind-finite cardinality: equinumerous with an initial
                segment $\{0, 1, \dots, n-1\}$ of $\mathbb{N}$ for some
                $n \in \mathbb{N}$."
Id: "88d1e87d-f649-4246-b417-7d3ca4c7f24c"


Text: "When an infinite set can be placed in one-to-one correspondence with the
       totality of natural numbers $\mathbb{N}$, its elements can be arranged in
       an exhaustive sequence, defining the first infinite cardinality
       $\aleph_0$:"
Id: "7f2e20f7-9700-4143-91c3-ef33ca489141"


[\(countably.infinite)::set]
Refines: X
satisfies: X \.equinumerous.with./ \naturals
Documented:
. adjective: "countably infinite"
. description: "Denumerable cardinality $\aleph_0$: equinumerous with the full
                set of natural numbers $\mathbb{N}$, admitting an exhaustive
                infinite sequence listing."
Id: "7337910a-2ecc-46f7-95a7-9cc5812af19e"


Text: "Combining finite sets and countably infinite sets yields the class of
       countable sets:"
Id: "88b7f640-5a5a-4435-a973-296d802663a2"


[\(countable)::set]
Refines: X
satisfies:
. anyOf:
  . X is? \(finite)::set
  . X is? \(countably.infinite)::set
Documented:
. adjective: "countable"
. description: "At most countable cardinality: $|X| \le \aleph_0$, being either
                finite or denumerably infinite."
Id: "9725d8a1-0c65-491e-901b-ee23b8e87888"


Text: "A natural question is whether cardinal dominance behaves as a partial
       order: if $A$ injects into $B$ and $B$ injects into $A$, must there exist
       a bijection between them? Remarkably, this holds without requiring the
       Axiom of Choice, as proved by Cantor, Felix Bernstein, and Ernst
       Schröder:"
Id: "99dbff35-92d4-4a5a-8db0-3d80f675f102"


Theorem:
given: A, B is \set
where:
. A \.cardinality.leq./ B
. B \.cardinality.leq./ A
then: A \.equinumerous.with./ B
Documented:
. called: "Cantor--Schroeder--Bernstein Theorem"
. description: "Antisymmetry of cardinal dominance: mutual injective embeddings
                $A \hookrightarrow B$ and $B \hookrightarrow A$ guarantee the
                existence of a bijection $A \cong B$, without requiring Choice."
References:
. $enderton.set.theory:page{143}
Id: "5ad0453f-1203-4467-949b-4c8a8697b5b3"


Text: "While $\mathbb{N}$, $\mathbb{Z}$, and $\mathbb{Q}$ all share the same
       countable cardinality $\aleph_0$, Cantor demonstrated that not all
       infinities are equal. The following landmark theorem proves by diagonal
       argument that no set can be placed in bijection with its own power set,
       establishing that $|\mathcal{P}(A)| > |A|$ unconditionally:"
Id: "2ad7a192-3850-48e0-bb14-9988b1ccf201"


Theorem:
given: A is \set
then:
. not: A \.equinumerous.with./ \power.set:of{A}
Documented:
. called: "Cantor's Theorem"
. description: "The foundational strict cardinal inequality
                $|A| < |\mathcal{P}(A)|$: diagonal argument proving no
                surjection $A \twoheadrightarrow \mathcal{P}(A)$ can exist,
                establishing an unending transfinite hierarchy of infinities."
Proof: """Suppose for contradiction that there exists a bijection
          $f : A \to \mathcal{P}(A)$. In particular, $f$ must be surjective.
          Define the diagonal subset $D \subseteq A$ by Separation:

          $$
          D := \{x \in A : x \notin f(x)\}
          $$

          Because $D \subseteq A$, $D$ is
          an element of the power set $\mathcal{P}(A)$. Since $f$ is surjective
          onto $\mathcal{P}(A)$, there must exist an element $d \in A$ such that
          $f(d) = D$. Now ask whether $d \in D$:
          - If $d \in D$, then by definition of $D$, $d \notin f(d) = D$, which
            is an immediate contradiction.
          - If $d \notin D$, then $d \notin f(d)$, which by definition of $D$
            implies $d \in D$, again a contradiction. Thus
            $d \in D \iff d \notin D$, which is a logical impossibility.
            Therefore no surjective function from $A$ to $\mathcal{P}(A)$ can
            exist, proving that $A$ is not equinumerous with $\mathcal{P}(A)$."""
References:
. $enderton.set.theory:page{132}
Id: "83a30248-2172-45d8-934a-e7a54be66b12"


Text: "Cantor's diagonal argument is one of the grand turning points in
       intellectual history. It demonstrates that infinity is not a single
       monolith: there exists an unending, strictly increasing hierarchy of
       infinite cardinalities:

       $$
       |A| < |\mathcal{P}(A)| < |\mathcal{P}(\mathcal{P}(A))| < \dots
       $$

       Moreover, the diagonal technique introduced here is the conceptual
       ancestor of the most profound limitative results of modern thought:
       Bertrand Russell's refutation of naive comprehension, Kurt Gödel's First
       Incompleteness Theorem, and Alan Turing's proof of the undecidability of
       the Halting Problem all derive their power from this same
       self-referential diagonal architecture."
Id: "8d163eb8-a148-43d1-80b3-38c54e51e278"
